Branch report on practical patient guidance

For patients, the practical guidance is to recognize when to see a doctor, what to expect from treatment and recovery, and when to avoid antibiotics and what to do instead.

How to recognize when to see a doctor for a viral vs bacterial infection depends on symptoms and duration. Viral infections typically present with symptoms such as runny nose, sore throat, fatigue, and mild fever, and most viral illnesses resolve on their own [N5-R5]. Viral infections are often systemic, and symptoms can include coughing, sneezing, fever, inflammation, vomiting, diarrhea, fatigue, and cramping [N5-R1]. Bacterial infections, by contrast, are often localized, and the classic symptoms are local redness, fever, swelling, and pain at the site of infection [N5-R4]. Viral infections may persist longer than the expected 10–14 days that a virus tends to last, and fever may be higher than one might expect from a virus [N5-R2]. A doctor may be needed when symptoms are severe, when they persist longer than expected, or when they suggest a bacterial infection such as a urinary tract infection, strep throat, or pneumonia [N5-R1][N5-R2][N5-R4]. The Mayo Clinic source also notes that it can be hard to tell whether a bacterial or viral infection is causing symptoms, so a careful review of symptoms and lab tests may be needed to find the right treatment [N3-F3].

What to expect from treatment and recovery varies by infection type. For bacterial infections, antibiotics may be used to kill or block bacterial growth, but the immune system is still needed to clear the infection [N4-F4]. For viral infections, antivirals may help the body clear out some viruses, but they often depend on the immune system and may not work as effectively as antibiotics [N3-F3]. The evidence also emphasizes that antivirals are not a replacement for the immune system and that many antivirals are made to target the virus rather than the host cell [N3-F3]. In addition, some viral infections, such as HIV, can be treated with antivirals, but viruses can develop resistance over time, especially in people with weakened immune responses [N3-F3]. For patients, recovery from viral infections is often slower and may require supportive care, while bacterial infections may respond more directly to antibiotics if the diagnosis is correct [N3-F3][N5-R5].

When to avoid antibiotics and what to do instead is a key patient message. The evidence is clear: antibiotics do not work against viral infections [N3-R4][N6-R4]. Patients should take antibiotics only when they are needed and only for bacterial infections [N6-R1]. Using antibiotics for viral infections or for mild bacterial infections that would go away on their own can lead to antibiotic resistance and unnecessary harm [N6-R2]. The CDC recommends taking antibiotics only when needed and not skipping doses, not saving your antibiotics, and not stopping them too early [N6-R5]. If a patient has a viral infection such as the common cold or flu, the correct response is to avoid antibiotics and instead focus on rest, hydration, and symptom management [N6-R4]. For viral infections that do not resolve on their own, the doctor may prescribe antivirals if available, but the evidence suggests that these are not universally effective and may still depend on the immune system [N3-F3].

Practical patient guidance, therefore, is:
- Viral infections: see a doctor if symptoms are severe, persistent, or unusual, and avoid antibiotics;
- Bacterial infections: antibiotics may be needed, but only when the doctor confirms they are appropriate;
- Recovery: expect supportive care for viral infections and immune-driven clearing for bacterial infections;
- Antibiotic misuse: avoid antibiotics for viral infections and never stop a prescribed course too soon [N5-R1][N5-R2][N5-R4][N3-R4][N6-R1][N6-R2][N6-R4][N6-R5].

